pseudo-Boolean proof version 2.0
* Regression test: two `transitivity` blocks in one order definition.
*
* Not a wrong verdict on its own -- and this used to be ACCEPTED at exit 0.
* It is refused now because the format admits each block of an order
* definition once, in one position, and the second block proves its
* obligations against the same definition as the first while overwriting the
* `transitivity_proven` flag the first set. Whatever the second block proves,
* it is the flag that the order's `end` reads, so "the last block wins" is the
* rule nothing stated. Found by the sweep that followed the
* transitivity-before-`def` wrong verdict; see
* incorrect/version2/pre_order_transitivity_before_def.
f 4
pre_order simple
    vars
        left u1
        right v1
        aux
    end
    def
        -1 u1 1 v1 >= 0 ;
    end
    transitivity
        vars
            fresh_right w1
        end
        proof
            proofgoal #1
                p -2 -3 + -1 +
            end -1
        end
    end
    transitivity
        vars
            fresh_right w1
        end
        proof
            proofgoal #1
                p -2 -3 + -1 +
            end -1
        end
    end
end
output NONE
conclusion NONE
end pseudo-Boolean proof
